L = lesson(c3, "human-rights", "Human rights",
    ["Define human rights and list their characteristics", "Name the main categories of rights and the main international texts", "Describe the rights of the child, of women and of other groups", "Explain how rights are protected and violated"],
    [("text", "**Human rights** are the basic rights and freedoms that every person has simply because they are human. They are **universal** (for everyone), **inalienable** (cannot be taken away), **indivisible** and equal. They protect human dignity. They are limited only by law and by the need to respect the rights of others."),
     ("key", "retenir", "International texts", "The **Universal Declaration of Human Rights** was adopted by the United Nations General Assembly on **10 December 1948**; that day is **Human Rights Day**. The **Convention on the Rights of the Child** was adopted by the UN in **1989**. In Africa the **African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights** (adopted in 1981) protects rights across the continent. The Constitution of Cameroon also refers to human rights in its preamble."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Rights of the child", "Children have the right to a **name and nationality**, to **care and protection**, to **education**, to **health**, to **play**, to be **protected from abuse, exploitation and harmful work**, and to **give their opinion**. Girls and boys have the same rights; discrimination is wrong."),
     ("text", "**Protection and violations.** Rights are protected by laws, courts, national and international institutions, associations and the media. Violations include torture, arbitrary arrest, child labour, trafficking, discrimination, violence against women and denial of education. Citizens can protect rights by knowing them, reporting violations, helping victims and respecting the rights of others."),
     ("key", "pieges", "Common mistakes", "- Human rights are not gifts from the government: they belong to every person.\n- Rights are not the same as wishes: they are recognised in law.\n- Do not forget that every right of one person means a duty of respect for others.")],
    [("Example 1: identify the violation", "A 10-year-old child is made to work all day in a quarry and does not go to school. Which rights are being violated?", ["The child has the right to education.", "The child has the right to protection from harmful work.", "Both are violated by this situation."], "The right to education and the right to protection from harmful work."),
     ("Example 2: universal rights", "Explain why human rights are called universal.", ["They apply to every human being.", "They do not depend on sex, religion, ethnic group, wealth or nationality.", "That is why they are the same in all countries."], "Because they apply to everybody, everywhere.")],
    (fig, "Four groups of rights", "Four boxes: civil and political, economic and social, cultural rights and rights of the child."),
    [MCQ("Human Rights Day is celebrated on", "10 December", ["1 January", "20 May", "11 February"], "It commemorates the 1948 Universal Declaration."),
     TF("Human rights apply to every person.", True, "They are universal."),
     MATCH("Match each right with its group.", [("The right to vote", "Civil and political right"), ("The right to work", "Economic and social right"), ("The right to use one's language", "Cultural right")], "Rights are grouped in categories."),
     MCQ("Which text protects children's rights?", "Convention on the Rights of the Child", ["Treaty of Versailles", "Berlin Treaty", "Ten-year plan"], "It was adopted in 1989.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Explain three ways in which human rights can be protected in a community.", "By teaching people their rights, by reporting violations to authorities or associations, by supporting victims, and by respecting others' rights and treating everyone equally.", "1 mark each for three ways with explanation.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A girl in a village is told that she must leave school to marry, although she wants to continue.",
          [("mcq", "Which right is concerned?", "The right to education", ["The right to a car", "The right to be famous", "The right to tax"], "She has the right to education.", {}),
           ("tf", "Girls and boys have the same right to education.", True, "Rights apply to both.", {}),
           ("mcq", "Who can help protect her rights?", "Family members, teachers, community leaders and authorities", ["Only strangers", "Nobody", "Only the army"], "Many actors can help.", {}),
           ("open", "Suggest two peaceful actions to help her.", "Talk to her parents and community leaders, inform teachers or social services or associations, and encourage dialogue about the benefits of education.", "1 mark each for two peaceful actions.", {})])],
    [("The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was adopted in", "1948", ["1884", "1960", "1998"], "10 December 1948."),
     ("Which is a child's right?", "Education", ["Child labour", "Abuse", "Neglect"], "Education is a right."),
     ("Human rights are", "Universal and inalienable", ["Valid only for adults", "For citizens of rich countries only", "Valid for one year"], "Universal means for everyone."),
     ("Child labour in dangerous conditions is a", "Violation of rights", ["Right", "Duty", "Tradition protected by law"], "It harms children."),
     ("The African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights dates from", "1981", ["1945", "1963", "2002"], "It was adopted in 1981.")],
    ["Teacher to verify: 10 December 1948 (UDHR), 1989 (UN Convention on the Rights of the Child), 1981 (African Charter), the preamble reference in the Constitution and the lists of rights. Cases are hypothetical."])

fig = shapes([CIRCLE(150, 105, 62, fill="lightblue"), CIRCLE(250, 105, 62, fill="lightgreen"), T(110, 100, "English", size=14, bold=True), T(290, 100, "French", size=14, bold=True), T(200, 106, "Cameroon", size=13, bold=True), T(200, 24, "Two official languages, many local languages", size=13)], 400, 190)
L = lesson(c3, "unity-diversity", "National unity, diversity and bilingualism",
    ["Define national unity and diversity", "Explain bilingualism in Cameroon and its advantages", "Describe obstacles to national unity such as discrimination and prejudice", "Suggest ways of promoting national integration"],
    [("text", "Cameroon has a great **diversity**: many peoples, languages, traditions and religions, and varied landscapes. **National unity** means that people of different origins feel part of one nation and work together for the common good. **National integration** is the process of bringing together the different groups, regions and cultures of the country in a common national life."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Bilingualism", "Cameroon has two official languages, **English and French**, which are used in government, education and the media; its history includes both British and French administration. Bilingualism helps communication, access to wider markets and education, international relations and understanding between citizens. Many citizens also speak local languages and Cameroonian Pidgin English, and some speak other languages as well."),
     ("key", "retenir", "Promoting unity", "Respect for others' culture and language; learning each other's language; national events, sports and symbols; school exchanges and mixed communities; fair treatment and equal opportunities in education, employment and public services; dialogue; and cooperation in development projects."),
     ("text", "**Obstacles:** tribalism and discrimination, prejudice and stereotypes, favouritism, unequal development between regions, misunderstandings between language communities, hate speech and rumours, and unresolved conflicts. Each of these weakens trust and can lead to conflict."),
     ("key", "attention", "Be careful", "- Diversity is a strength, not a problem.\n- Unity does not mean that everyone must be the same: it means respecting differences within a common nation.\n- Use respectful language about every community; avoid generalising about any group.")],
    [("Example 1: advantage", "Give two advantages of being able to speak both English and French in Cameroon.", ["Bilingual people can communicate with more citizens across the country.", "They can find more jobs, study further and communicate with more countries.", "It also builds understanding between communities."], "Wider communication across the country and more opportunities."),
     ("Example 2: stereotype", "A pupil says, 'All people from that region are lazy.' Explain what is wrong with this statement.", ["It is a generalisation about a whole group.", "Individuals differ and the statement is a stereotype that fuels prejudice.", "Such statements harm national unity."], "It is a harmful stereotype that damages unity and respect.")],
    (fig, "Two official languages and a common nation", "Two overlapping circles labelled English and French, with Cameroon in the overlap."),
    [MCQ("Cameroon's two official languages are", "English and French", ["French and Portuguese", "English and Spanish", "Hausa and French"], "These are the official languages."),
     TF("National unity means that everybody must be exactly the same.", False, "It means respecting differences within one nation."),
     MATCH("Match each term with its meaning.", [("Tolerance", "Accepting differences"), ("Discrimination", "Unfair treatment of a group"), ("Integration", "Bringing groups together in national life")], "These words belong together."),
     MCQ("Which action promotes national unity?", "Learning the other official language", ["Spreading rumours about a community", "Refusing to speak with neighbours", "Insulting others' customs"], "Learning promotes understanding.", tier="approfondissement"),
     OPEN("Give three ways in which schools can promote unity and bilingualism.", "Teach both languages, organise mixed activities and exchanges between pupils from different regions and language backgrounds, celebrate cultural diversity, apply fair rules and stop discrimination.", "1 mark for each of three ways with explanation.", points=3, tier="approfondissement"),
     PROB("A new pupil from another region joins a school in Yaoundé. Some classmates laugh at her accent, but others welcome her and invite her to join a group.",
          [("mcq", "Laughing at her accent is", "disrespectful and may be discrimination", ["a good way to help her", "a school rule", "a tradition"], "Mockery harms dignity.", {}),
           ("tf", "A welcoming attitude helps integration.", True, "It builds friendships and trust.", {}),
           ("mcq", "The teacher should", "Encourage respect and cooperation", ["Ignore the problem", "Punish the new pupil", "Separate pupils by region"], "A teacher should promote respect.", {}),
           ("open", "Give two things the class could do to make everyone feel included.", "Group work with mixed teams, sharing foods and stories from different regions, learning words in each other's languages and having clear rules against mockery.", "1 mark each for two sensible actions.", {})])],
    [("National integration means", "Bringing groups together in one national life", ["Separating groups", "Closing schools", "Banning languages"], "Integration builds unity."),
     ("Diversity means", "Variety of peoples, languages and cultures", ["One culture only", "Only cities", "Only farms"], "Cameroon is diverse."),
     ("A stereotype is", "A fixed, oversimplified idea about a group", ["A fact", "A law", "A tax"], "Stereotypes lead to prejudice."),
     ("A way to promote unity is", "Dialogue and respect", ["Hate speech", "Favouritism", "Rumours"], "Dialogue builds trust."),
     ("Bilingualism helps", "Communication between citizens", ["Isolation", "Conflict", "Poverty only"], "It helps people understand each other.")],
    ["Teacher to verify the statements about the official languages and the roles of Pidgin and national languages. The wording is deliberately neutral and avoids political positions; any classroom discussion should be moderated."])

